===============================================================================
I. BASIC SETUP: HETEROGENEOUS STATISTICAL EXPERIMENTS
===============================================================================

Let Z be a latent state space. For a finite selected channel portfolio S, each channel
p in S is a stochastic kernel

    K_p(. | z),    z in Z,

from latent states into a channel-specific observation space X_p.

The central DCSG question is:

    Which distinctions, functions, geometries, and decision-relevant quantities on Z
    remain simultaneously representable/stable across every selected lossy channel?

The theory has two related layers:

(A) exact identifiability: equality of channel-output laws;
(B) graded identifiability: pseudometrics/distances between channel-output laws.

===============================================================================
II. EXACT IDENTIFIABILITY AND THE PARTITION-LATTICE FORMULATION
===============================================================================

For channel p define observational equivalence

    z ~_p z'
    iff
    K_p(. | z) = K_p(. | z').

Let pi_p be the corresponding partition of Z.

For a selected portfolio S, define the exact common equivalence relation as the
equivalence closure / transitive closure of the union of all channel equivalences:

    ~_S^*  :=  EqClosure( union_{p in S} ~_p ).

Equivalently, in the lattice of partitions ordered by refinement,

    Q_S^* = join_{p in S} pi_p.

Here pi <= sigma means pi refines sigma (every block of pi is contained in a block
of sigma). Thus Q_S^* is the finest partition coarser than every channel partition.

CORE EXACT IDENTIFIABILITY THEOREM (FND-T01)

A scalar function f: Z -> R is separately identifiable from every channel p in S
iff f is constant on every block of Q_S^*.

Equivalently:

    f(z) = f(z')
    whenever z ~_S^* z'.

Interpretation:
different channels can contribute different equality edges, and transitive chaining
across channels can destroy exact distinctions that no single channel destroys alone.

Important:
the partition-lattice join itself is classical. DCSG's use of it is as the exact
common-identifiability object generated by heterogeneous experiments.

===============================================================================
III. FROM EXACT EQUALITY TO GRADED COMMON GEOMETRY
===============================================================================

Choose for each channel a decision-relevant pseudometric/distance between output laws:

    d_p(z,z') := D_p( K_p(.|z), K_p(.|z') ).

Typical examples can include total variation, Hellinger, or another declared
experiment/decision-relevant discrepancy.

Define the pointwise common edge envelope

    m_S(z,z') := min_{p in S} d_p(z,z').

This minimum need not satisfy the triangle inequality.

Define its finite-chain path closure

    d_S^*(x,y)
      :=
      inf over finite paths x=x_0,...,x_n=y
      sum_{i=0}^{n-1} m_S(x_i,x_{i+1}).

CORE COMMON-GEOMETRY THEOREM (GEO-T02)

d_S^* is the greatest pseudometric dominated by every channel distance d_p.

More explicitly:

1. d_S^* <= d_p for every p in S;
2. if delta is any pseudometric satisfying delta <= d_p for every p,
   then delta <= d_S^*.

Reason:
delta <= min_p d_p = m_S on each edge, then the triangle inequality for delta gives
delta(x,y) <= every path sum, hence <= the infimum.

This construction is a min-plus / shortest-path metric closure. That generic closure
machinery is classical. The DCSG object is the specific common geometry obtained from
heterogeneous statistical experiments.

REJECTED ALTERNATIVE

Thresholding d_p or min_p d_p and taking connected components is NOT a valid generic
graded common geometry. Chaining can merge endpoints that remain directly very far.
Without a certified separation gap, threshold-components lose graded information.

===============================================================================
IV. EXACT FUNCTIONAL DUALITY
===============================================================================

Define the common stable scalar class

    F_S
      :=
      { f: Z -> R :
        |f(x)-f(y)| <= d_p(x,y)
        for every p in S and every x,y }.

Then

    d_S^*(s,t)
      =
      sup_{f in F_S} |f(s)-f(t)|.

The upper bound follows by summing the channel-Lipschitz constraints along paths.
Equality is attained constructively by the distance potential

    f_s(z) := d_S^*(s,z).

Thus d_S^* is exactly the largest separation simultaneously expressible by a scalar
function that is 1-Lipschitz in every channel geometry.

===============================================================================
V. EXACT LIPSCHITZ NORM INTERSECTION
===============================================================================

For a pseudometric d define

    ||f||_Lip(d)
      :=
      sup_{x != y, d(x,y)>0}
      |f(x)-f(y)| / d(x,y),

with the usual convention that a nonzero numerator across a zero-distance pair gives
infinite seminorm.

CORE NORM IDENTITY (GEO-T25)

    ||f||_Lip(d_S^*)
      =
      max_{p in S} ||f||_Lip(d_p).

Equivalently, the unit ball is exactly

    B_Lip(d_S^*)
      =
      intersection_{p in S} B_Lip(d_p).

Proof sketch:

Since d_S^* <= d_p,

    ||f||_Lip(d_p) <= ||f||_Lip(d_S^*)

for every p.

Conversely let

    L = max_p ||f||_Lip(d_p).

Then for every edge,

    |f(x)-f(y)| <= L d_p(x,y)   for every p,

hence

    |f(x)-f(y)| <= L min_p d_p(x,y) = L m_S(x,y).

Sum along paths and take the infimum:

    |f(x)-f(y)| <= L d_S^*(x,y).

Therefore equality holds.

This result is algebraically central because it says the common geometry is not just
an arbitrary shortest-path object: its Lipschitz space is exactly the intersection
of the channel Lipschitz spaces with the max seminorm.

===============================================================================
VI. DUAL MOLECULE / ARENS–EELLS DECOMPOSITION
===============================================================================

On a finite latent space, let mu be a zero-total-mass molecule

    mu = sum_z a_z delta_z,     sum_z a_z = 0.

Let ||mu||_{F(d)} denote the Arens–Eells / Lipschitz-free norm dual to the
basepointed Lipschitz seminorm induced by d.

CORE DUAL DECOMPOSITION (GEO-T26)

    ||mu||_{F(d_S^*)}
      =
      inf_{mu = sum_{p in S} mu_p}
      sum_{p in S} ||mu_p||_{F(d_p)},

where every mu_p is itself zero-total-mass.

Network interpretation:
mass transport may choose a channel separately on each transported edge and may
change channel at intermediate latent states. The common transport geometry is
therefore a channel-labelled transshipment problem.

This identity is the dual of the primal norm-intersection structure.
The general convex-analytic mechanism (intersection/max norm <-> infimal convolution)
and Lipschitz-free machinery are classical; the channel-labelled DCSG specialization
is the object of interest.

===============================================================================
VII. CHANNEL PRICES / WEIGHTED COMMON GEOMETRY
===============================================================================

Let alpha = (alpha_p)_{p in S} with alpha_p > 0 be declared channel prices/costs.

Define

    m_{S,alpha}(x,y)
      :=
      min_{p in S} alpha_p d_p(x,y),

and let d_{S,alpha}^* be its path closure.

Then the weighted Lipschitz identity is

    ||f||_Lip(d_{S,alpha}^*)
      =
      max_{p in S}
      [ ||f||_Lip(d_p) / alpha_p ].

On the dual side,

    ||mu||_{F(d_{S,alpha}^*)}
      =
      inf_{mu = sum_p mu_p}
      sum_p alpha_p ||mu_p||_{F(d_p)}.

Define the semantic value of molecule mu at price alpha by

    V_mu(alpha)
      :=
      ||mu||_{F(d_{S,alpha}^*)}.

Because V_mu is an infimum of linear functions of alpha over feasible channel-use
decompositions, it is:

    - monotone in alpha;
    - positively homogeneous;
    - concave;
    - piecewise linear / polyhedral in the finite case.

===============================================================================
VIII. SEMANTIC USAGE SET AND SUPPORT-FUNCTION GEOMETRY
===============================================================================

For a fixed molecule mu, associate to each feasible channel-routed decomposition a
nonnegative usage vector

    c = (c_p)_{p in S},

where c_p is the channel-p contribution/cost before multiplying by alpha_p.

Let U_mu be the closed convex upward hull of all feasible usage vectors.

Then

    V_mu(alpha)
      =
      inf_{c in U_mu} alpha · c,
      alpha >= 0.

Hence U_mu can be reconstructed from the value oracle:

    U_mu
      =
      { c >= 0 :
        alpha · c >= V_mu(alpha)
        for all alpha >= 0 }.

This is a lower-support representation of a closed convex upward set.

ACTIVE PROFILES / SUPERDIFFERENTIAL

If c is active at alpha, i.e.

    V_mu(alpha) = alpha · c,

then c is a supergradient of the concave function V_mu at alpha.

Under finite/polyhedral conditions, the superdifferential is the convex hull of
active usage profiles. Price space is partitioned by the lower normal fan of U_mu.

Positive homogeneity gives an Euler-type identity at differentiable points:

    V_mu(alpha) = alpha · grad V_mu(alpha).

This is the algebraic basis of semantic tomography:
query V_mu(alpha), recover active usage profiles, reconstruct the decision-equivalent
usage geometry.

The support-function / normal-fan machinery is classical convex geometry.
Any novelty claim must be in the DCSG reduction and its statistical/discovery laws,
not in support-function theory itself.

===============================================================================
IX. NOVELTY RESIDUAL AND COMPLETENESS
===============================================================================

Suppose D is a discovered set of usage profiles. Let U_D be the convex upward hull
generated by D, and let

    V_D(alpha)
      :=
      inf_{c in U_D} alpha · c.

Because U_D is generated from only discovered profiles,

    V_D(alpha) >= V(alpha).

Define the semantic novelty residual

    R_D(alpha)
      :=
      V_D(alpha) - V(alpha)
      >= 0.

Then:

1. R_D(alpha) > 0 certifies that at least one undiscovered route/profile improves the
   current discovered envelope at alpha.

2. R_D == 0 on the full positive price domain iff the discovered profiles generate
   the same decision-equivalent closed convex upward usage hull as the true system.

A global incompleteness quantity is

    E(D)
      :=
      sup_{alpha in Delta_{P-1}} R_D(alpha).

Finite-net, Lipschitz, PAC, and anytime certificates were developed on top of this
algebraic residual. Those are statistical layers rather than new algebraic primitives.

