X. BANACH-VALUED EXTENSION
===============================================================================

Let Y be a normed/Banach output space and F: Z -> Y.

Define

    ||F||_Lip(d;Y)
      :=
      sup_{x != y}
      ||F(x)-F(y)||_Y / d(x,y).

Then the scalar norm-intersection argument extends:

    ||F||_Lip(d_S^*;Y)
      =
      max_p ||F||_Lip(d_p;Y).

Dual scalarization by Y* and the universal factorization through the
Lipschitz-free/Arens–Eells space are classical functional analysis and are NOT
claimed as new generic theorems.

The DCSG interpretation is:
vector-valued semantics does not create extra common identifiability; it packages
scalar stable probes under a declared codomain norm.

===============================================================================
XI. CONTINUUM / STABILITY BOUNDARY
===============================================================================

Finite latent spaces admit a hop-count bound for simple paths, so a uniform additive
edge perturbation epsilon yields a path-closure error bound of order

    (|Z|-1) epsilon.

In dense/infinite spaces there is no uniform finite hop bound. Uniform additive edge
accuracy alone can therefore collapse path geometry under refinement.

The theory distinguishes:

(A) additive fidelity:
    |hat d_p - d_p| <= eta;

(B) multiplicative/relative fidelity:
    (1-eps)d_p <= hat d_p <= (1+eps)d_p.

Metric/path closure is monotone and positively homogeneous, so multiplicative fidelity
passes through the closure without hop-count amplification:

    (1-eps) d_S^*
      <=
      hat d_S^*
      <=
      (1+eps) d_S^*,

provided the channel edge envelopes satisfy the same relative fidelity.

This is a boundary result, not a claim that relative estimation is easy.
Near true zero distances, relative estimation is statistically much stronger and
requires additional separation/local-SNR structure.

===============================================================================
XII. STATISTICAL RECOVERY OF THE EXACT ZERO RELATION
===============================================================================

Assume a separated regime:

    d_p(x,y) in {0} union [Delta, infinity)

for all p,x,y, with Delta > 0.

Suppose estimated pair distances satisfy a simultaneous additive event

    sup_{p,x,y} |hat d_p(x,y) - d_p(x,y)| <= eta.

If

    2 eta < Delta,

then thresholding at eta exactly recovers every channel zero edge:

- if d_p(x,y)=0, then hat d_p(x,y) <= eta;
- if d_p(x,y)>=Delta, then
      hat d_p(x,y) >= Delta-eta > eta.

Therefore the union/transitive closure recovers the exact common quotient.

UNKNOWN-GAP VERSION

The estimator need not know Delta. It can always use the confidence-derived threshold
eta. Once the unknown true gap eventually satisfies 2 eta < Delta, the conservative
outer object automatically becomes exact.

MULTINOMIAL-TV SPECIALIZATION

For P channels, Z latent states, k output categories, and N iid observations per
channel-state row, a conservative finite-alphabet L1/TV concentration argument yields

    r_N
      =
      sqrt( [k log 2 + log(PZ/alpha)] / (2N) )

for simultaneous row-TV error.

Pairwise TV-distance error is then at most

    eta_N = 2 r_N.

The sufficient exact-recovery condition

    2 eta_N < Delta

becomes

    4 r_N < Delta,

hence

    N >
    8 [k log 2 + log(PZ/alpha)] / Delta^2.

This is explicitly a conservative distribution-free sufficient bound.

