XIII. PROVISIONAL OLD-LAB EXTENSION: NO-GAP IMPOSSIBILITY
NOT YET CANONICAL
===============================================================================

Consider a one-channel two-state Bernoulli subproblem:

Null:
    K(a) = K(b) = Ber(1/2).

Alternative:
    K(a) = Ber(1/2),
    K(b) = Ber(1/2 + epsilon), epsilon > 0.

The exact quotient differs between null and every alternative.

PROVISIONAL T64

If the model class allows arbitrarily small epsilon > 0, then for every finite N,

    inf_{quotient estimators}
    sup_M P_M( Qhat_N != Q_M^* )
    >= 1/2.

Reason:
Le Cam two-point testing; for fixed finite N, the N-sample laws converge in TV as
epsilon -> 0.

Interpretation:
- fixed positive gap -> pointwise eventual exact recovery can occur;
- no lower gap over the model class -> no uniform finite-sample exact recovery;
- finite stability of the estimated partition does not by itself certify exact equality.

This lower-bound engine is classical Le Cam theory; only the DCSG reduction is project-specific.

