XIV. PROVISIONAL EPISTEMIC EXTENSION: FEASIBLE QUOTIENT SET
NOT YET CANONICAL
===============================================================================

Let C(D) be a joint confidence set for the entire heterogeneous channel family.

For each feasible K in C(D), compute its exact common quotient Q(K).

Define the feasible quotient family

    FrakQ(D)
      :=
      { Q(K) : K in C(D) }.

Define partition-lattice envelopes

    Q^-(D)
      :=
      meet_{Q in FrakQ(D)} Q,

    Q^+(D)
      :=
      join_{Q in FrakQ(D)} Q.

If the true channel family K* belongs to C(D), then automatically

    Q^-(D) <= Q* <= Q^+(D)

in refinement order.

Interpretation:

Q^-:
    mergers/equivalences that hold in every confidence-compatible quotient.

Q^+:
    least upper partition containing every confidence-compatible quotient;
    all mergers that remain possible after taking lattice closure.

Important:
Q^+ need NOT itself be the quotient of any single feasible model.
It is an epistemic upper envelope, not necessarily a feasible ontology.

If

    Q^- = Q^+,

then the exact common quotient is self-certified on the confidence event.

This does not contradict the no-gap impossibility: the interval need not collapse
at any finite sample size near an exact-equality boundary.

SEQUENTIAL MONOTONICITY

For nested confidence sets

    C_{t+1} subseteq C_t,

the envelopes obey

    Q^-_t
      <=
      Q^-_{t+1}
      <=
      Q^+_{t+1}
      <=
      Q^+_t.

Thus evidence contracts the partition-lattice uncertainty interval monotonically.

With an anytime-valid confidence sequence, the stopping time

    tau = inf{ t : Q^-_t = Q^+_t }

is an anytime-valid exact self-certification rule whenever it stops.

General set-valued inference and partition lattices are classical. The DCSG-specific
composition above is provisional and requires novelty audit.

