XV. PROVISIONAL SHARP UPPER ENVELOPE
NOT YET CANONICAL
===============================================================================

For a nonempty model/confidence set C, define the existential equality graph G_exists:

    {x,y} is an edge
    iff
    there exists K in C and channel p
    such that K_p(.|x) = K_p(.|y).

PROVISIONAL T69

    Q^+ = CC(G_exists).

Reason:

    Q^+
      = join_{K in C} Q(K)
      = join_{K in C} join_p pi_p(K),

and a join of equivalence relations is the equivalence closure / connected-components
partition of the union of all equality edges.

Different edges may come from different feasible models; this is allowed because
Q^+ is a lattice join, not a jointly feasible quotient.

ROW-RECTANGULAR CONFIDENCE

If

    C = product_{p,x} C_{p,x},

then an equality edge is existentially feasible iff

    C_{p,x} intersect C_{p,y} != empty

for some channel p.

Therefore

    Q^+
      =
      CC(
        union_p {
          {x,y} :
          C_{p,x} intersect C_{p,y} != empty
        }
      ).

TV-BALL SPECIALIZATION

If

    C_{p,x}
      =
      B_TV( Khat_p(x), r_{p,x} ),

then, because the probability simplex is convex,

    C_{p,x} intersect C_{p,y} != empty

iff

    TV(Khat_p(x),Khat_p(y))
      <=
      r_{p,x}+r_{p,y}.

Hence

    Q^+
      =
      CC{
        {x,y} :
        exists p,
        d_hat_p(x,y) <= r_{p,x}+r_{p,y}
      }.

This sharpens the earlier "possible-zero outer relaxation":
for rectangular TV confidence balls it is proposed to be the SHARP upper
partition-lattice envelope, although still not necessarily a feasible exact quotient.

===============================================================================
XVI. PROVISIONAL LOWER ENVELOPE: ROBUST CONNECTIVITY
NOT YET CANONICAL
===============================================================================

A tempting symmetric construction fails:

    "take equality edges that hold in every feasible model, then connected components."

Counterexample:
three states a,b,c; two channels.

Channel 1:
    a=0, b=0, c=1.

Channel 2:
    a=0, b=1, c in {0,1}.

Every feasible model merges all three states in the common quotient:
channel 1 connects a-b, while channel 2 connects c to either a or b.

But the only individually forced equality edge is a-b.

Thus

    CC(forced edges) < Q^-

can be strict.

So Q^- is a robust connectivity object:
a pair is merged only if every confidence-compatible world connects it,
possibly through different paths in different worlds.

CUT CHARACTERIZATION (PROVISIONAL T71)

For states s,t:

    s ~_{Q^-} t

iff there is NO feasible model K and NO cut A with

    s in A, t notin A

such that no channel equality edge of K crosses A | A^c.

Equivalently:

    s not~_{Q^-} t

iff at least one feasible model separates s and t in its common quotient.

This is essentially the cut dual of "connected in every feasible union graph."

