XVII. PROVISIONAL COMPUTATIONAL HARDNESS CLAIM
NOT YET CANONICAL — PLEASE AUDIT CAREFULLY
===============================================================================

Finite rectangular candidate-label model:

Each channel-state row is allowed to take one deterministic output label from an
explicit finite candidate set.

Decision problem SEPARABLE:

    Is there a confidence-compatible channel family whose common quotient separates
    designated states s,t?

PROVISIONAL T72 CLAIM

SEPARABLE is NP-complete.
Therefore deciding

    s ~_{Q^-} t

is coNP-complete.

Proposed reduction source:
Set Splitting / Hypergraph 2-Colorability (Property B).

Reduction sketch:

Given hypergraph H=(V,E), create states

    s, t, l_e, r_e   for every e in E

and two channels.

Channel 0 (pinning):
use deterministic labels L,R:

    s and every l_e -> L,
    t and every r_e -> R.

Channel 1:
s,t receive private labels unused elsewhere.
For each hyperedge e,

    l_e may choose any vertex-label v in e,
    r_e may choose any vertex-label v in e.

A feasible model separates s,t iff the set of labels chosen on the left is disjoint
from the set chosen on the right.

This is proposed to be equivalent to a 2-coloring of V in which every hyperedge meets
both colors:

- given a valid 2-coloring, choose for each l_e a color-0 vertex in e and for each r_e
  a color-1 vertex in e;

- given disjoint left/right selected label sets, color left-used labels 0, right-used
  labels 1, unused vertices arbitrarily. Every hyperedge has at least one selected
  label of each color.

Membership in NP is immediate by guessing one allowed deterministic label for every
row and checking s-t disconnection.

THIS CLAIM IS PROVISIONAL.
Independent reviewers should attempt to break the reduction, especially:
- unintended equality paths;
- labels reused across hyperedges;
- whether arbitrary coloring of unused vertices can violate an edge;
- whether the pinning channel introduces unintended cross-connections;
- whether the exact problem definition matches standard Set Splitting hardness.

